  "content": "Eight Verses of Training the MindMay I always hold all sentient beings dear,With the intention to accomplish the supreme goal,Which is more precious than a wish-fulfilling jewel,For all sentient beings.Whenever I associate with anyone,May I view myself as the lowest of all,And from the depths of my heart,May I hold others as supreme and most dear.In all my actions, may I examine my own mind,And the moment an affliction arises—Since it harms both myself and others—May I forcefully confront and avert it.When I see beings of a wicked nature,Oppressed by intense negativity and suffering,May I cherish them as if I had found a precious treasure,For they are so difficult to find.When others, out of jealousy,Treat me unjustly with abuse, slander, and the like,May I accept the defeat myself,And offer the victory to others.Even if someone whom I have helped,And in whom I have placed great hope,Should harm me in an extremely unjust manner,May I see them as a sublime spiritual teacher.In short, may I offer all benefit and happinessTo all mothers, both directly and indirectly;And may I secretly take upon myselfAll the harm and suffering of my mothers.May all these practices be unstainedBy the impurities of the eight worldly concerns,And through the mind that realizes all phenomena are like an illusion,May I be liberated from the bonds of attachment.Composed by the Kadampa Geshe Langri Tangpa Dorje Sengé.",
